      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that two randomly chosen members are both girls, we can use the formula for probability: probability = favorable outcomes over total outcomes. \n\nThere are 5 choices for the first girl and 4 choices for the second girl. So, there are 5 * 4 = 20 ways to choose two girls.\n\nThere are 10 total members, so there are 10*9/2 = 45 total ways to choose two members. \n\nTherefore, the probability that two randomly chosen members are both girls is 20/45, which can be simplified to 4/9. $\\boxed{\\frac{4}{9}}$.\n\nEvaluate $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^5$. Express your answer as a common fraction.\n\nTo evaluate \\(\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^5\\), we will use the property of exponents and repeatedly multiply the fraction by \\( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\).\n\n1. Start with the given expression:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^5\n   \\]\n\n2. Recognize that raising a fraction to a power can be rewritten as raising both the numerator and the denominator to that power:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^5 = \\dfrac{3^5}{4^5}\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(3^5\\) and \\(4^5\\):\n   \\[\n   3^5 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 243\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4^5 = 4 \\times 4 \\times 4 \\times 4 \\times 4 = 1024\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute these values back into the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^5 = \\dfrac{243}{1024}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^5\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{243}{1024}}\\).\n\nIf z = √[ 3 + 4i ] + √[-3 - 4i ], calculate |z| and the argument of z.\nAnswer Choices: (A) 5/2 45° (B) 5/2 60° (C) 5/2 15° (D) 3 30° (E) None of the above\n\nLet's use polar form for this expression. Let z = a + ib, then |z| = √(Real(Z)² + Imaginary(Z)²) and to caculate the argument in radians: (Argument = tan ¹ (Imaginary(Z) / Real(Z))),.\n1. |z| = 5/2 \n2. Argument of z = 45° = 45/180 = 0.25 THe answer is A",
